## How a Migration Engagement Works

The migration programme has three phases: before, during, and after launch. Each phase has a defined evidence set and approval point.

**Before launch**, I benchmark performance, crawl the current site, build or review the URL inventory, validate the redirect map, and compare important content. I then crawl staging and give your development team a prioritised issue list with acceptance checks.

**During launch**, I coordinate with your existing developers rather than replacing them. They manage the platform and deployment. I verify the SEO requirements, test the live response, and identify issues by URL, template, and severity. Nothing goes live without sign-off on the redirect map.

**After launch**, I monitor Google Search Console, GA4, crawl data, indexation, and priority rankings. Your team receives concise actions tied to the evidence, so developers can fix technical faults quickly and marketing can avoid changing pages while signals are still settling.

## Migration Questions

### Will I lose rankings when I change platform?

Some movement is normal while Google recrawls the new site. Losses are mostly avoidable. Every indexed URL needs a redirect. Ranking content needs to carry over. The launch needs checking the day it happens. The loss comes from missing redirects, stripped content and launch faults. The platform itself is not the cause. My own move from WordPress to Next.js is the example above.

### What does a migration audit include?

The £495 migration audit covers:

- A redirect map of every indexed URL.
- Historic URLs found through backlinks and web archives. Old links with value are not lost.
- A list of the titles, H1s and content to keep on each priority page.
- A staging crawl before launch.

Launch-day checks and the 30, 60 and 90-day monitoring are scoped to the project on top of the audit.

### Can you fix a migration someone else already did?

Yes. Recovery after another provider's migration is a common enquiry. I compare the live site with what Google had indexed before the move. Search Console, backlink data and archives show the gaps. Then I find the URLs that lost a redirect, content or a canonical. Your developer gets a prioritised list. The scope is quoted after the free 20-minute diagnosis.

### Do you make the changes or does my developer?

Your developer makes the changes. They manage the platform and deployment. I write the requirements, test staging and the live site, and give them the fixes in priority order. With no developer, I can scope implementation separately after the diagnosis.

### Can it be ready before a fixed launch date?

Tell me the date in the free 20-minute diagnosis. I confirm whether the audit and fixes fit before it. I say so when the date is too close. Timing depends on site size and how much is changing. It is quoted after the diagnosis.

### How long does a website migration affect SEO?

A planned migration keeps the disruption short and any fault traceable. A migration with no redirect map can leave pages invisible for much longer. The length depends on how much changes and how well the old signals carry across. The 30, 60 and 90-day checks show whether recovery is on track.

### Do I need an SEO consultant if my developer is handling the migration?

Yes. Developers preserve function. Rankings depend on SEO-specific tasks such as the redirect map, content parity checks and the Google Search Console handover. An SEO consultant owns those checks and works alongside the development team.

### Should I change domain and redesign at the same time?

No. Sequence them so each change can be measured and any problem can be isolated. Complete the migration first when both changes are unavoidable. Release the redesign after rankings and indexation have stabilised.

